<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">UniECG: Understanding and Generating ECG in One Unified Model</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiarui Jin
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haoyu Wang
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>$⋆$ Equal Contribution. $†$ Corresponding authors.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiang Lan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jun Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gaofeng Cheng
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hongyan Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shenda Hong
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent unified models such as GPT-5 have achieved encouraging progress on vision-language tasks. However, these unified models typically fail to correctly understand ECG signals and provide accurate medical diagnoses, nor can they correctly generate ECG signals. To address these limitations, we propose UniECG, the first unified model for ECG capable of concurrently performing evidence-based ECG interpretation and text-conditioned ECG generation tasks. Through a decoupled two-stage training approach, the model first learns evidence-based interpretation skills (ECG-to-Text), and then injects ECG generation capabilities (Text-to-ECG) via latent space alignment. UniECG can autonomously choose to interpret or generate an ECG based on user input, significantly extending the capability boundaries of current ECG models. Our code and checkpoints will be made publicly available at <a href="https://github.com/PKUDigitalHealth/UniECG" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/PKUDigitalHealth/UniECG</a> upon acceptance.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>ECG, unified model, multi-modal large language model, MLLM

</div><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span>
<sup id="id2.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup> Peking University, China 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id2.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> National University of Singapore, Singapore
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id2.3" class="ltx_sup">3</sup> University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, China 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
hongshenda@pku.edu.cn, leehy@pku.edu.cn

</span></span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The electrocardiogram (ECG) has become a crucial tool in the clinical diagnosis of cardiac diseases, owing to its non-invasive nature, low cost, and ease of acquisition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. While deep learning methods have shown promising results in tasks such as cardiac anomaly detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and rare ECG synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, these methods are typically designed for either classification or generation under specific clinical constraints. Consequently, they often struggle to perform both tasks simultaneously and lack crucial capabilities for dialogue with interpretability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in unified models for vision-language tasks offer a promising avenue to overcome the limitations of conventional ECG analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. However, existing unified models, such as GPT-5, fail to achieve satisfactory performance on both ECG understanding and generation. For ECG understanding, these models tend to generate responses that appear plausible and contextually relevant, yet are factually incorrect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. For ECG generation, they are limited to producing images that often contradict the given conditions and lack accurate, fine-grained temporal features. Furthermore, their inability to generate signal-level data renders the image-only output incompatible with downstream deep learning models, precluding applications like data synthesis for class-imbalanced datasets. Although recent ECG-specific multimodal large language models have advanced language-based interpretation<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, their capabilities are confined to understanding and lack generative functionality, thus hindering their application across a broader spectrum of tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address the aforementioned challenges, we propose UniECG, the first unified model tailored for ECG. UniECG is uniquely capable of both interpreting ECG images and signals to provide evidence-based diagnoses, and generating high-fidelity ECG signals with fine-grained temporal features from textual conditions. This dual functionality significantly extends the application boundaries of current large multimodal models for ECG. The training of UniECG proceeds in two distinct stages: (1) Learning to Understand ECG, and (2) Learning to Generate ECG. In the first stage, the model acquires its evidence-based understanding capabilities through full-parameter fine-tuning, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ UniECG: Understanding and Generating ECG in One Unified Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. In the second stage, we endow the model with generative functionality by aligning the embeddings of its special [ECG] tokens with those from the DiffuSETS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> text encoder in latent space. During inference, UniECG dynamically responds to user input by either generating an evidence-based textual explanation or yielding special [ECG] tokens. These special tokens are subsequently fed into the DiffuSETS decoder to synthesize signal-level ECG data. In summary, our primary contributions are as follows:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.18588v1/figs/framework.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/267;" width="476" height="267" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Framework of UniECG.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce UniECG, the first unified model tailored for ECG that concurrently addresses two critical tasks: evidence-based ECG understanding and text-conditioned ECG generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a two-stage training strategy to decouple the ECG understanding and generation tasks. The model first learns evidence-based interpretation ability (ECG-to-Text), then injects ECG generation (Text-to-ECG) ability through latent space alignment, while largely retaining its initial understanding ability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments show that UniECG, while largely retaining its evidence-based ECG interpretation ability, demonstrates remarkable ECG generation ability, effectively extending the capability boundaries of current ECG models.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
