<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Learning to Understand ECG</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an ECG signal <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, an ECG image <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and its textual caption <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, our goal is to fully fine-tune a large language model (LLM) such that it can jointly reason over ECG data and textual inputs, thereby generating clinically meaningful responses grounded in ECG interpretation. To achieve this, we leverage the ECG-Grounding dataset, which contains evidence-based ECG diagnostic dialogues. Specifically, we first tokenize the textual caption into a sequence of tokens <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{1},\ldots,y_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{1},\ldots,y_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, modality-specific backbones are employed to extract signal embeddings <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\phi}(s)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\phi}(s)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and visual embeddings <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\phi}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\phi}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. To align these representations with the LLM input space, two linear mappings, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{ts}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{ts}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{tsi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{tsi}</annotation></semantics></math>, are introduced to project the signal and visual embeddings into <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vectors, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the input embedding dimension of the LLM. Finally, we jointly optimize <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{ts}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{ts}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{tsi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{tsi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the LLM parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> on image–signal–text triplets by minimizing the negative log-likelihood of the target textual sequences:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{c}(s,x,y_{1:T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{c}(s,x,y_{1:T})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log p_{\theta}\Big(y_{t}\,\Big|\,[\,w_{\phi}(s)\mathbf{W}_{ts}^{\top}\mathbf{W}_{tsi}^{\top};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>;</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log p_{\theta}\Big(y_{t}\,\Big|\,[\,w_{\phi}(s)\mathbf{W}_{ts}^{\top}\mathbf{W}_{tsi}^{\top};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle v_{\phi}(x)\mathbf{W}_{tsi}^{\top}\,],\,y_{1:t-1}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{\phi}(x)\mathbf{W}_{tsi}^{\top}\,],\,y_{1:t-1}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Learning to Generate ECG</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the GILL framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we extend the LLM vocabulary with special [ECG] tokens, whose final-layer hidden states are designated as the representations for driving ECG generation. To enhance the expressiveness of the LLM in ECG generation, we employ <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[ECG_{1}],\ldots,[ECG_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[ECG_{1}],\ldots,[ECG_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math>, to represent the ECG output. Specifically, after adding these special tokens to the vocabulary, we freeze the entire LLM and only update the gradients of the newly added tokens in the input embedding matrix, which we define as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{ECG}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>ECG</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{ECG}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We train the model by minimizing the negative log-likelihood of generating the correct sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[ECG_{1}]\ldots[ECG_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[ECG_{1}]\ldots[ECG_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on previously generated tokens, thereby enabling the model to learn when to generate the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[ECG]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[ECG]</annotation></semantics></math> tokens and how to arrange them properly:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{e}(y)=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log p_{\{\theta\cup E_{\text{ECG}}\}}\big([{\rm ECG}_{i}]\mid s_{1:t}\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>ECG</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ECG</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{e}(y)=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log p_{\{\theta\cup E_{\text{ECG}}\}}\big([{\rm ECG}_{i}]\mid s_{1:t}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During training, we construct the training set using textual reports from MIMIC-IV-ECG. Specifically, we convert each textual report in the dataset into two forms: query and answer. For the query, the format is “Generate an ECG of …”, and for the answer, the format is “This is an ECG of …”, where the ellipsis is replaced by the corresponding textual report. The special <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[ECG]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[ECG]</annotation></semantics></math> tokens are appended to the end of the answer and used for subsequent ECG signal generation. It is worth noting that this process does not require any ECG data, but relies solely on text-based training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For ECG signal generation, we adopt DiffuSETS, a diffusion-based framework for text-guided ECG synthesis. DiffuSETS with nomic-embed-text-v1.5 as the text encoder is employed for the experiments. The key idea is to project the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[ECG]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[ECG]</annotation></semantics></math> tokens into a semantically meaningful region of DiffuSETS’ input space, which is achieved via a lightweight mapper. Specifically, the mapper is implemented as an encoder–decoder Transformer with trainable parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, taking as input the final-layer hidden states of the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[ECG_{1}]\ldots[ECG_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[ECG_{1}]\ldots[ECG_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> sequence from the LLM and querying them with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> learnable query embeddings <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. To enforce semantic alignment, we minimize the mean squared error between the mapper’s outputs and the embeddings generated by the frozen text encoder of DiffuSETS:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{d}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{d}(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Big\|f_{\omega}\big(h_{\{\theta\cup E_{\text{ECG}}\}}(y,[ECG_{1}]),\ldots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>ECG</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Big\|f_{\omega}\big(h_{\{\theta\cup E_{\text{ECG}}\}}(y,[ECG_{1}]),\ldots,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle h_{\{\theta\cup E_{\text{ECG}}\}}(y,[ECG_{n}]),q_{1:L}\big)-T_{\psi}(y)\Big\|_{2}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>ECG</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{\{\theta\cup E_{\text{ECG}}\}}(y,[ECG_{n}]),q_{1:L}\big)-T_{\psi}(y)\Big\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the loss function of the second training stage is formulated as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{g}(y)=\mathcal{L}_{e}(y)+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{d}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{g}(y)=\mathcal{L}_{e}(y)+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{d}(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 1.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Inference Stage</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During inference stage, UniECG automatically determines how to respond based on user input. For ECG understanding tasks, UniECG performs evidence-based diagnosis according to the given ECG data. When the user requests ECG generation, the model outputs special <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[ECG]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[ECG]</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, which are then fed into the mapper to obtain mapped vectors. Finally, these vectors are passed into the pretrained DiffuSETS decoder to generate ECG signals conditioned on the corresponding text.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.18588v1/figs/dialogue.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/242;" width="476" height="242" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Dialogue example of UniECG.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiment and Analysis</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We primarily employ the ECG-Grounding and MIMIC-IV-ECG datasets for training and validation in UniECG understanding and ECG generation tasks. The descriptions of these two datasets are provided as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MIMIC-IV-ECG.</span> This publicly available dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> provides a comprehensive collection of 800,035 multiple-lead ECG recordings from 161,352 subjects. Each recording was sampled at a rate of 500 Hz and has a duration of 10 seconds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ECG-Grounding.</span> This publicly available dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> is derived from MIMIC-IV-ECG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and comprises  30,000 instruction–response pairs annotated with heartbeat-level features (e.g., QRS, PR, QTc, ST), providing the first fine-grained benchmark for evidence-driven ECG interpretation to enhance explainability and trustworthiness. Finally, the evaluation results are scored by DeepSeek V3.1.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.3 Inference Stage ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ UniECG: Understanding and Generating ECG in One Unified Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the detailed dialogue workflow of UniECG. The left-hand panel demonstrates UniECG’s ECG understanding capability, whereby it performs evidence-based ECG interpretation and diagnosis given a user-provided electrocardiogram. Red text highlights the key evidential features, blue text denotes the final diagnostic conclusions, and the ellipsis indicates less critical information. The right-hand panel showcases UniECG’s generative ability, enabling it to produce signal-level ECGs from textual instructions. For example, when asked to generate an ECG with atrial fibrillation, UniECG produces a clinically standard waveform characterized by irregular R–R intervals. Furthermore, it can flexibly adjust the generated ECG according to user-specified attributes such as heart rate, sex, and age.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ UniECG: Understanding and Generating ECG in One Unified Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ UniECG: Understanding and Generating ECG in One Unified Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> present the quantitative results on ECG understanding tasks, further highlighting the advantages of our method in clinical interpretability. Since the second stage involves fine-tuning the LLM input embedding layer, a certain degree of knowledge forgetting is inevitable, which leads to slightly lower overall performance compared to the current best GEM. However, relative to PULSE, UniECG demonstrates substantial improvements across multiple dimensions. For instance, ECG feature grounding improves from 41.63 to 69.44, evidence-based reasoning from 38.53 to 61.76, and the overall average from 39.95 to 63.98. These improvements indicate that UniECG not only preserves most of the evidence-chain–based reasoning ability but also enhances interpretive consistency and stability in complex clinical contexts. Moreover, UniECG not only preserves strong ECG understanding but also uniquely enables signal-level ECG generation, a capability absent in other models, thereby extending the frontier of current approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce UniECG, the first unified model for ECG that is capable of performing both evidence-based ECG understanding and text-guided ECG generation. We propose a two-stage training strategy that equips existing ECG MLLMs with both interpretive and generative capabilities, thereby effectively expanding the capability frontier of current ECG models. While we acknowledge that UniECG still has notable limitations in both understanding and generation, we hope our approach will inspire the community to further explore the capability boundaries of next-generation ECG foundation models.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
